  "worked_solution": "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/0d9e032bfffa68f487347d6c169999e313247e89ef7de3fae28edb216dcf718f.png)",
